Found while implementing #9 (git_index_find_prefix), which sits directly next
to this coverage. Not fixed there to keep that change surgical.

t/20-index.t:51-56 tests the out-param of git_index_find like this:

    my $find_idx_buf = "\0" x 8;
    my ($find_idx_ptr) = scalar_to_buffer($find_idx_buf);
    my $rc_find = Git::Libgit2::FFI::git_index_find( $find_idx_ptr, $index, "testfile.txt" );
    my $find_idx = unpack( "Q", $find_idx_buf );
    is( $find_idx, 0, "git_index_find returned index 0" );

The binding declares that parameter as size_t*. Passing a plain integer
(the address from scalar_to_buffer) instead of a scalar ref does NOT make
FFI::Platypus write through that address -- verified empirically against
libgit2 1.5.1 with git_index_find_prefix, same signature shape:

    ptr  "tasks/"  rc=0  buffer_after=0     # real answer is 1
    ref  "tasks/"  rc=0  pos=1              # scalar ref works

So the buffer stays at its "\0" x 8 initialisation and `is($find_idx, 0)`
is vacuously true: it passes no matter what git_index_find does with the
position. The assertion cannot fail, which makes it worse than no coverage
-- it reads as if the out-param were pinned.

Fix: pass a scalar ref, as the POD synopsis for git_index_find already
shows and as the new git_index_find_prefix coverage does:

    my $pos;
    is( Git::Libgit2::FFI::git_index_find( \$pos, $index, "testfile.txt" ), 0, ... );
    is( $pos, 0, ... );

Then make the position assertion able to fail -- with one entry in the
index the only valid position is 0, so add a second entry (or reuse the
prefix repo) and assert a non-zero position for it.

Worth a grep for the same scalar_to_buffer-for-a-pointer-out-param pattern
elsewhere in t/; git_index_write_tree and git_blob_create_from_buffer use
it too, but those take opaque, not a typed pointer, so they are fine. The
suspect set is exactly the typed pointer params (size_t*, int*, ...).
Fixed in the working tree (uncommitted).

t/20-index.t: the git_index_find block now passes a scalar ref, and the
index carries a second entry ("aaa.txt", which sorts before "testfile.txt")
so position 0 is no longer the only possible answer -- testfile.txt must
come back at 1. Added a GIT_ENOTFOUND miss case, the "out-param untouched
on a miss" assertion, and an undef/NULL at_pos case. The extra entry is
removed again right after the block so the linear index state of the rest
of the script is unchanged.

Falsifiability verified by mutation, not asserted:
  a) old raw-pointer form restored -> GOT 0, CHECK 1, FAIL
  b) expected position 1 -> 0        -> GOT 1, CHECK 0, FAIL
  c) GIT_ENOTFOUND -> 0              -> GOT -3, CHECK 0, FAIL
File restored after each; prove -lr t/ green (26 files, 5231 tests).

Root cause pinned down while doing this: FFI::Platypus passes NULL, not the
integer, when a non-ref is handed to a pointer-to-native param. Probe:
git_libgit2_version (int*, int*, int*) does not NULL-check its args and
segfaults (exit 139) when given a raw scalar_to_buffer address. That covers
every typed pointer param, opaque* included.

Grep for the same pattern across t/ (signature-aware scan of all 522
FFI call sites against the 234 parsed _attach signatures, multi-line calls
included) found three more, all with a non-ref for a *-typed param:
  - t/13-tag.t:73 git_reference_create -- fixed here, see below
  - t/16-config.t:31 git_config_get_string (string*) -> ticket #12
  - t/34-cherrypick-revert.t:69/89 git_cherrypick_commit / git_revert_commit
    (opaque*) -> ticket #13
The three unparsed signatures (git_commit_create, git_libgit2_opts, ffi)
were checked by hand: git_commit_create arg0 is plain opaque, which is the
correct house pattern for a caller-allocated git_oid.

t/13-tag.t:70-76 was fixed in the same pass because it is unambiguous:
git_reference_create arg0 is git_reference** (opaque*), but the test handed
it a 20-byte git_oid buffer and then asserted
like(oid_to_hex($tag1_buf), qr/\A[0-9a-f]{40}\z/). With NULL passed, the
buffer stayed zeroed and oid_to_hex returned 40 zeros, which matches the
regex -- vacuous (verified with a standalone probe). Now takes the handle
with a scalar ref, asserts git_reference_target equals the first commit
OID, and frees the reference; mutating the expected OID to $c2_hex makes it
red.
